<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harriet Tubman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1822–<a href="/years/1913">1913</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Araminta Ross</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harriet Tubman was an American abolitionist and social activist who was born into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in Dorchester County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a>. After escaping to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> in 1849, she made some thirteen missions back to rescue about seventy enslaved people, among them members of her family, through the network known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/underground-railroad">Underground Railroad</a>. During the Civil War she served the Union Army as an armed scout and spy, and guided the raid at Combahee Ferry in 1863, which freed more than 700 people. In her later years she worked for women's suffrage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harriet-tubman.jpg" alt="Harriet Tubman" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Horatio Seymour Squyer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harriet_Tubman_(circa_1885).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
